"A relational schema diagram documents tables, column types, keys, and enforced relationships in a specific database design.\n\nUse when: Review migrations and referential integrity. Help developers write joins against the implemented schema.\n\nAvoid when: The model must remain independent of a database implementation.\n\nVisual grammar: Table cards show columns and selected data types. Primary, unique, and foreign-key markers distinguish constraints. Connectors map referencing column sets to referenced keys.\n\nBuild: Record the database engine, schema, and revision. Build table cards from verified DDL or mark them proposed. Connect compound keys as ordered column sets. Place frequently joined tables nearby and keep foreign-key connectors out of column text.\n\nCheck: Foreign keys point to valid unique or primary keys. Nullability and uniqueness match the actual constraints. Composite-key ordering and delete actions are explicit where relevant. Table headers and column rows have adequate padding and readable connector anchors.\n\nPitfalls: Implying a relationship is enforced when it is only an application convention. Omitting compound-key members and changing the apparent join.\n\nExample: order_line references orders through order_id and products through product_id, with its declared primary key shown.\n\nAlternatives: Entity relationship diagram, Object-relational mapping diagram."
